.title  What the 386 changed: registers, addresses, instructions
.what   The map lecture before writing for the 386, in four small programs, one growing; each asks first whether this is an 80386 (ENGINE\E_CPU.INC, lesson 24's detector) and then prints the 8086's way and the 386's side by side. R1 the registers: 32 bits, the low half keeps the 8086's name, the top half has none, FS and GS; 100000 + 50000 is ADD and ADC on the 8086 and one ADD on the 386. R2 the addresses: any register, times 1, 2, 4 or 8, and LEA as a calculator. R3 the new instructions: MOVZX, MOVSX, IMUL into any register, SETcc, BSR. R4 what did not change: one address an instruction - memory to memory still goes through a register - and real mode's 1 MB and 64 KB segments. The 186 and 286 are passed over
.tier   LESSON
.main   R4.ASM
.needs  R1.ASM R2.ASM R3.ASM
.build  FASM R1.ASM R1.COM
.build  FASM R2.ASM R2.COM
.build  FASM R3.ASM R3.COM
.build  FASM R4.ASM R4.COM
.run    R4.COM
.uses   MOVZX MOVSX IMUL SETL BSR LEA ADC CBW MOVSD
.engine ENGINE\E_CPU.INC
